-- title:
--   Cancellation of invertible ideal sheaves
-- statement:
--   Let $X$ be a scheme and let $I$, $J$, $J'$ be elements of `X.IdealSheafData`, i.e. quasi-coherent ideal sheaf data on $X$ (an assignment of an ideal of $\Gamma(X,U)$ to each affine open $U$, compatible under localisation), multiplied pointwise. Assume $I$ satisfies the predicate `IsInvertible`: for every point $x$ of $X$ there are an affine open $U \subseteq X$ and a section $f \in \Gamma(X, U)$ with $x$ in the basic open $X.\mathrm{basicOpen}\, f$, together with an element $g$ of the monoid of non-zero-divisors of $\Gamma(X, D(f))$, where $D(f)$ denotes the affine basic open `X.affineBasicOpen f` determined by $f$, such that the ideal attached to $D(f)$ by $I$ is the principal ideal $\mathrm{span}\{g\}$. Assume further that $I \cdot J = I \cdot J'$ as ideal sheaf data. The conclusion is $J = J'$. Thus an ideal sheaf which is locally generated by a single non-zero-divisor on a basic-open cover may be cancelled from a product of ideal sheaf data.
--
--   This is the cancellation law making invertible (locally principal, locally generated by a non-zero-divisor) ideal sheaves cancellative for the multiplication of ideal sheaf data; in classical terms, effective Cartier divisors add cancellatively. It is used in the treatment of relative effective Cartier divisors and the relative Picard functor, for instance in [`AlgebraicGeometry.RelEffCartierDiv.exists_split`](thm.html#AlgebraicGeometry.RelEffCartierDiv.exists_split) and in the results on relative sub-Picard presheaves and zero cuts.
